# Forbidden strings — what the git hooks keep out of the public repo.
#
# Copy this to scripts/git-hooks/forbidden_strings.txt (gitignored) and fill in
# YOUR real personal data + secrets. The live file is NEVER committed — it
# contains the very strings we keep out of the public repo.
#
# Three entry formats (blank lines and #-comments ignored; parsed by forbidden_list.sh):
#   <substring>     a contact detail: case-INSENSITIVE substring match. Use for
#                   emails, a street address — specific enough to avoid false
#                   positives (a full email, not a common word).
#   phone:<number>  a phone number, 10 digits, written any way. Matches the number
#                   in any common format: 310 555 0100, (310) 555-0100,
#                   310.555.0100, 3105550100.
#   word:<Word>     case-SENSITIVE whole-WORD match. Use for personal names and
#                   the hardcoded assistant name. A capitalized assistant-name
#                   entry blocks it used as a bare display string but NOT
#                   lowercased identifiers / import paths, so it catches the name
#                   hardcoded as display text, not code references. A whole-word
#                   entry also won't match inside a longer word.
#
# Where each kind is checked:
#   pre-commit  all three, against the ADDED lines of the staged diff.
#   pre-push    contact details and phone numbers, against EVERY tracked file of
#               the commit being pushed (catches what is already in the tree, and
#               anything committed with --no-verify). Names are not checked at
#               push time.
#
# Examples (REPLACE with your own — these are placeholders):
# you@example.com
# spouse@example.com
# phone:555-555-0123
# 123 Main St, Springfield
# word:YourName
# word:Aria        # your assistant's name, to catch it hardcoded as a string
